<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Danishmendids</h1><p class="st-meta">1071 CE – 1178 CE (approx.) · capital Sivas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Turkish dynasty of central and north-eastern Anatolia that rivalled the Seljuks of Rum and inspired the epic of Danishmend Gazi.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Seljuk victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Manzikert</a> in 1071, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> leader known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmend-gazi">Danishmend Gazi</a>, whose origins are obscure, conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niksar">Niksar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melid">Malatya</a>, founding one of the first Turkish principalities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Danishmendids ruled a largely Christian population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> warriors and a growing urban Muslim elite. In 1100 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmend-gazi">Danishmend Gazi</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-i-of-antioch">Bohemond of Antioch</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gumustekin-gazi">Gazi Gumushtigin</a> was for a time the strongest ruler in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, receiving the title malik from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. They built mosques and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokat">Tokat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niksar">Niksar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a>. Their deeds were later celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendname">Danishmendname</a>, a Turkish epic of holy war.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yagibasan">Yagibasan</a> in 1164 the family split and weakened. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilij-arslan-ii">Kilij Arslan II</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> around 1174 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/melid">Malatya</a> branch in 1178, ending the dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/danishmendids.jpg" alt="Danishmendids" width="320" height="167"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anatolia_1097.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1071 CE – 1178 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sivas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Danishmend Gazi, Gazi Gümüştigin, Yağıbasan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Turkish, Persian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Danishmend dynasty, Danishmendid Emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1124&amp;focus=danishmendids" aria-label="Danishmendids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5593 755 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Danishmendids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5913 1011L6481 1127M5913 1011L5810 1072"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6481 1127h0M5810 1072h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5913 1011h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5913 1011h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sivas. <a href="/atlas?year=1124&amp;focus=danishmendids">On the